  "topic": "Template Function for Operator Overloading",
  "explanation": "In C++, templates enable the creation of generic functions and classes that can operate with any data type. When overloading operators, templates allow defining behavior for user-defined types or container classes in a type-independent manner. This promotes code reusability and type safety, especially in the context of standard library containers like std::multimap, where operators such as '!=' can be overloaded to compare container instances generically.",
  "syntax": "template <class Key, class T, class Compare, class Allocator>\nbool operator!=(const multimap<Key,T,Compare,Allocator>& x, const multimap<Key,T,Compare,Allocator>& y);",
